{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The heavens recount</b> — This psalm is very esteemed, and it is bound with the work of the heavens. Now I shall explain it in brief fashion, except that whoever has not studied the wisdom of the constellations will not understand it." "<b>The heavens</b> — The motion of the spheres, which are equal in one respect and different in another, and which proceed in a straight path in themselves, but it is not straight relative to the inhabitants of the earth. And the measure of motion neither increases nor decreases in absolute demonstrations, but it increases and decreases in visual appearance. Now, whoever knows the ways of the circles knows the knowledge of the Most High. And the heavens are like instructors teaching righteousness on a path, or 'Speak to the earth and it shall instruct you' [Job 12:8]. And behold, the heavens are the dwellings of the stars." "<b>And the word 'recount'</b> — Because they stand upon a single fixed arrangement, and this is the glory of HaShem. And it says 'El' — to make known His power." "<b>And the firmament tells</b> — That is the air, as I explained in the pericope of Genesis. Things are renewed every day, therefore it is written 'and the work of His hands,' for together with the heavens it [the firmament] declares the glory of El, and this is true, for the air receives the power of the ministers and the great host."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {1 "The phrase כמו מלמדים מורי צדק is textually difficult; translated hyper-literally. The paraphrase of Job 12:8 ('speak to the earth') appears to be Ibn Ezra's midrashic adaptation."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:27:52.271139916Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}